{"type":"result","subtype":"success","duration_ms":36078,"duration_api_ms":36078,"is_error":false,"result":"Reviewing the cache implementation and related expiry logic.\n\n\nRunning tests to confirm the failure, then fixing expiry logic.\n\n\nFixing the boundary bug (`age >= ttl`) and removing the unused legacy wrapper.\n\n\nThe problem was a mismatch between the documented TTL rule and the implementation.\n\n**Root cause:** `is_expired` used `age > ttl`, so an entry exactly at the TTL (e.g. 60s old with a 60s TTL) was still treated as fresh. The docstring says `age == ttl` should count as expired.\n\n**Changes in `repo/cache.py`:**\n- Use `age >= ttl` so the boundary matches the docstring and `cache_summary` in `rows.py` (which already relied on `is_expired`).\n- Removed the stale “BUG” comment that described the issue but didn’t match a fix path.\n- Removed unused `old_ttl_check`, which only forwarded to `is_expired` and was marked for post-migration removal.\n\nAll tests in `tests/visible/test_cache.py` pass.","session_id":"f99027ae-fce1-490b-aa01-05c66a62e378","request_id":"0902ca39-88e2-4e01-ab6b-d33f6a634912","usage":{"inputTokens":11672,"outputTokens":1416,"cacheReadTokens":89346,"cacheWriteTokens":0}}
